INT. JURY ROOM - FLOODLIT TENT - NIGHT

A federal courthouse flattened by a hurricane, so the court sits in a canvas tent on the parking lot. Folding table, plastic chairs, a single fan. Generator thrum outside. Rain on the roof.

GLORIA ACHTERBERG, 60s, foreperson, cardigan, a stack of index cards in front of her. DWAYNE "DEEZ" PRUITT, 30s, ball cap, sits arms crossed. MIN-JUN HAN, 40s, nurse, kneads her temples.

GLORIA
(to the room)
Let's take another vote. All in favor of conviction.

Deez doesn't raise his hand. Min-Jun hesitates. Gloria raises hers.

GLORIA (CONT'D)
One.

MIN-JUN
Gloria, I'm not sure--

GLORIA
You said yesterday you were.

MIN-JUN
I said I leaned.

DEEZ
I never leaned.

GLORIA
Deez, with respect, you haven't opened your mouth in two days.

DEEZ
I'm saving it.

GLORIA
For what?

DEEZ
For when it matters.

He stands. The chair scrapes. He crosses to a pitcher, pours water, drinks. Then, without warning, he slams the cup down.

DEEZ (CONT'D)
(to himself)
Twelve men on a bench and none of them looked at the kid.

MIN-JUN
Deez?

DEEZ
You heard me.

GLORIA
We are not here to rehash the trial.

DEEZ
Gloria, he's nineteen. He had a record of one parking ticket.

GLORIA
He had a gun.

DEEZ
He had a toy.

GLORIA
The witness said it was real.

DEEZ
The witness was a hundred yards away in the dark.

Min-Jun rises, steps between them, hands out.

MIN-JUN
Stop. Both of you.

DEEZ
She keeps calling it a verdict. It's a guess.

GLORIA
You think I haven't been losing sleep?

DEEZ
I think you made up your mind on day one.

Gloria slaps her cards down. They fan across the table. Deez knocks them to the floor with the back of his hand. Min-Jun snatches one before it's lost.

MIN-JUN
Enough! We have to be unanimous!

DEEZ
Then we're not leaving this tent.

GLORIA
(very quiet)
Then we're not leaving.

The generator coughs. The lights flicker. All three look up. The fan slows to a stop.

In the dark, no one moves.
